{:edb/db-before-t 2320 :edb/db-after-t 2321 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535633 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:06:31.678Z" 13194139535633 true] [17592186064231 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358510 13194139535633 true] [17592186064232 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358503 13194139535633 true] [17592186064233 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358514 13194139535633 true] [17592186289476 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/af84278943fcf2d9c4e3b99c115a4d82672fe50f2bfed8be7be84648985f80df.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" 13194139535633 true] [17592186289476 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/af84278943fcf2d9c4e3b99c115a4d82672fe50f2bfed8be7be84648985f80df.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" 13194139535633 false] [17592186289476 1145 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535633 true] [17592186289476 1145 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535633 false] [17592186358503 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358504 13194139535633 true] [17592186358503 1089 "q-103778" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358503 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535633 true] [17592186358503 1100 "A gas station pump dispenses gas into an empty gas tank at a rate of $\\frac{11}{6}t$ liters per second. Write an expression for $A(t),$ the total volume of gas in the tank at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time measured in second." 13194139535633 true] [17592186358503 1113 #uuid "470653e9-5d5b-542e-9020-2a9f1d9e16a0" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358503 1145 "Let $f(t)=\\frac{11}{6}t$ be the rate at which the gas pump dispenses gas, and let $A(T)$ be the total number of liters in the car after $T$ seconds.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{11}{6}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{11}{6}\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{11{T}^{2}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nA(t)=\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}.\n$$" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358505 13194139535633 true] [17592186358504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358506 13194139535633 true] [17592186358504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358507 13194139535633 true] [17592186358504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358508 13194139535633 true] [17592186358504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358509 13194139535633 true] [17592186358504 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535633 true] [17592186358504 1041 "selection" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358504 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358506 13194139535633 true] [17592186358504 1110 #uuid "41cf300e-79d9-5b26-9829-c540109921a4" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358505 1034 "A(t)=\\frac{5}{6}t+\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535633 true] [17592186358505 1111 #uuid "cd98d233-50ae-538f-9ab1-03b1028a79b1" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358506 1034 "A(t)=\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535633 true] [17592186358506 1111 #uuid "7a341548-1213-5609-9861-3793f4607179" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358507 1034 "A(t)=5+\\frac{5}{6}t" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535633 true] [17592186358507 1111 #uuid "a528bb8b-4f94-5c25-b4b3-b722bb5f74ed" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358508 1034 "A(t)=5+\\frac{5}{6}t+\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358508 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535633 true] [17592186358508 1111 #uuid "a58e441a-b64b-5736-8941-bf99e46494f7" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358509 1034 "A(t)=5+\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358509 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535633 true] [17592186358509 1111 #uuid "73d19e40-ba02-5c7b-a452-b6944ddfaffd" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358510 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358511 13194139535633 true] [17592186358510 1089 "q-346534" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358510 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535633 true] [17592186358510 1100 "Calculate $F(9)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F(9)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358510 1113 #uuid "9e6fc43c-b4e2-588d-9575-ad3c07d6f70b" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358510 1145 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=9,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(9)={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(9) & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{9}{t}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{t}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =12({9}^{1/2}-{1}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =12(3-1) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358512 13194139535633 true] [17592186358511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358513 13194139535633 true] [17592186358511 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535633 true] [17592186358511 1041 "field-1" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358511 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358513 13194139535633 true] [17592186358511 1110 #uuid "66c993e2-b41f-5743-b634-4b00f2295784" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358512 1034 "36" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535633 true] [17592186358512 1111 #uuid "f95ca8be-e73e-5244-a320-018dd4061aa5" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358513 1034 "24" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535633 true] [17592186358513 1111 #uuid "e7284f24-a13a-5561-8590-3d350cff79e4" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358514 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358515 13194139535633 true] [17592186358514 1089 "q-49308" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358514 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535633 true] [17592186358514 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/288b3eeaf1168316f2d8f43fa9484032daa8c9006a1c8355b8edfd1186dc2add.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-4}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358514 1113 #uuid "d89bb6ec-58ba-5dce-8d06-89f659d78ac4" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358514 1145 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/9f570d7f04bcf5645a97a6a1d85e72f602c436ef184e841fc4c998c0d5eb8e2e.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-4,-1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [-1,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-4,-1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [-1,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (1+2) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+4.5=10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358516 13194139535633 true] [17592186358515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358517 13194139535633 true] [17592186358515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358518 13194139535633 true] [17592186358515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358519 13194139535633 true] [17592186358515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358520 13194139535633 true] [17592186358515 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535633 true] [17592186358515 1041 "selection" 13194139535633 true] [17592186358515 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358520 13194139